{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>When a man among you brings an offering to HaShem.</b> It begins in singular language and concludes in plural language — ״תַּקְרִיבוּ קׇרְבַּנְכֶם״ ('you shall bring your offering'). And what Rashi explained — that it teaches that two people may volunteer an olah in partnership — does not suffice to account for the verse from beginning to end, for we would not know where the matter is interrupted. And it appears to me that He wished to warn all who bring an offering to HaShem that they not stumble in two things in which Cain and Abel stumbled, they being the first to bring an offering. For Cain stumbled in that he brought from the least and the inferior, namely flax seed; and those who sin in their souls need to bring the soul of an animal in exchange for their soul, and only under constraint did the Holy One, blessed be He, permit the poor man to bring a meal-offering that has no soul, and He accounts it for him as though he offered his own soul, as Rashi explained on the verse ״וְנֶפֶשׁ כִּי תַקְרִיב מִנְחָה״ (Leviticus 2:1). And Abel, even though he brought from the firstborn of his flock, nevertheless did not do well in that he was not stirred toward the offering on his own and did not rouse himself to this commandment on his own, but only after he saw that Cain had brought an offering did he take example from him, and Abel too brought, to make himself equal to him — as explained above in parashat Bereshit." "Therefore He said ״אָדָם כִּי יַקְרִיב מִכֶּם״ in singular language, to tell you that the one who brings should be like the first man in his offering, who was unique in the world. And just as on the one hand he was saved from robbery, for he was unique and everything was his, so on the other hand he was saved from the thing in which Abel stumbled — for since he was unique in the world, he was surely stirred toward the offering on his own and not because he saw others doing so. And of this He said <b>אָדָם</b> — when he is like the first man; <b>כִּי יַקְרִיב מִכֶּם</b> — that the bringing should be from you and not from others besides you, for from you and within you shall come the stirring toward the offering. This is <b>קׇרְבָּן לַה׳</b>, for surely His intent is none other than for the sake of HaShem — to exclude one who is not stirred from himself; this is neither ״מִכֶּם״ nor ״קׇרְבָּן לַה׳״. It is not ״מִכֶּם״, for behold, the stirring comes to him from others and not ״מִכֶּם״ from the one bringing the offering himself. And it is not ״קׇרְבָּן לַה׳״, for everything that is a man's jealousy of his fellow is done only to glorify himself before people, and it turns out that it is not an offering for the sake of HaShem but for the sake of people. And this is what they said (Shabbat 28b): the ox that the first man offered had a single horn on its forehead — therefore there was available to him an ox with a single horn, for it resembled the man who was a single horn, that is to say, he was unique in the world and robbed no man, and was not stirred by any man but only from himself, which is <b>מִכֶּם</b>; therefore his offering ascended for acceptance. It turns out that <b>קׇרְבָּן לַה׳</b> is where the matter is interrupted. And afterwards He said <b>מִן הַבְּהֵמָה</b> etc., to exclude flax seed, but rather one should do as Abel did, who brought from the firstborn of his flock. And what He said, <b>תַּקְרִיבוּ קׇרְבַּנְכֶם</b>, means from their fat — the choicest part that a person selects for himself, which is called <b>קׇרְבַּנְכֶם</b>; that you shall bring to the Most High. And He used <b>קׇרְבַּנְכֶם</b> in plural language, according to Rashi's explanation, to say that two people may bring one animal." "And there are those who say: ״אָדָם כִּי יַקְרִיב מִכֶּם״ — from his very body, through fasts and a broken spirit; this is ״קׇרְבָּן לַה׳״ and the portion of the Most High alone. But that which you bring ״מִן הַבְּהֵמָה מִן הַבָּקָר״ — this is ״קׇרְבַּנְכֶם״, for you are the ones who eat of the sacrifices of your well-being, the priests and the owners. Therefore ״קׇרְבַּנְכֶם״ is in plural language, for it is not the portion of the Most High alone." "<b>When a man brings.</b> Rashi explained: the matter speaks of freewill offerings. 'Why is אָדָם said? As the first man did not bring from robbery,' etc. Rashi first gave an explanation on ״כִּי יַקְרִיב״ and afterwards on the word ״אָדָם״, because at first ״אָדָם״ — why is it said? — does not pose a difficulty, for one could say that it comes to exclude the nations, who are not called אָדָם, since we do not accept from them an obligatory offering. But now that ״כִּי יַקְרִיב״ is said, which indicates that it is speaking of a freewill offering, and the Sages said (Nazir 62a): ״אִישׁ אִישׁ״ ('any man') — to include the nations, who vow vows and freewill offerings like Israel — if so, why is אָדָם said? Rather: as the first man did not bring from robbery. And the expression ״מִן הַגָּזֵל״ ('from robbery') needs explanation, for he should have said 'did not bring a robbed thing.' And it appears that Rashi's reasoning is this: for it is not the way of the world that a person would go and rob an animal and bring it to the Most High — for do we deal with cases before a court of law? And who is such a fool as to commit a transgression in order to fulfill a commandment with it, when he has no benefit from the transgression? However, this does occur: that a person robs much money or does injustice with much money, and he goes and gives part of that robbed money to charity or to an offering, with this calculation that he thought: through this he will have atonement for what remains in his hand. And this is the expression ״מִן הַגָּזֵל״ ('from the robbery') and not all of it, in the manner that they expounded ״מִן הַבְּהֵמָה״ ('from the animal') (Temurah 28b) and not all of it." "And we find that this parashah — at its beginning, its middle, and its end — is a warning against robbery. At its beginning: ״אָדָם כִּי יַקְרִיב״; at its middle: ״וְהֵסִיר אֶת מֻרְאָתוֹ בְּנוֹצָתָהּ״, because the bird sustains itself from robbery; and at its end, where it says ״וְהֵשִׁיב אֶת הַגְּזֵלָה״. For the offering and robbery are two opposites, for the offering draws near the Shekhinah and robbery distances it, as it says (Psalms 12:6): ״מִשֹּׁד עֲנִיִּים״ etc. ״עַתָּה אָקוּם יֹאמַר ה׳״ etc. And furthermore, one who offers his animal is as though he offered his own soul, for a man's property is accounted as his soul, for with his soul he brings his bread, as it is written (Deuteronomy 24:6): ״כִּי נֶפֶשׁ הוּא חֹבֵל״. And it is written (Proverbs 22:23): ״וְקָבַע אֶת קֹבְעֵיהֶם נָפֶשׁ״, and a part of the soul is as the whole. But one who offers a robbed animal, in which he invested no labor and toiled no effort — how can it be an exchange for his soul?" "<b>From the animal, from the cattle and from the flock.</b> There is room here for me to find words of substance and a reason for all the divisions of the offerings that come for atonement. For an anointed priest and the Sanhedrin — their offering is a bull; and the offering of an individual is a lamb or a goat; and in the case of one whose offering fluctuates, the poor person brings two turtledoves; and in extreme poverty, a tenth of an ephah of fine flour. For every sinner and rebel is as though he had horns to gore with them — whether upward or to the sides. For all transgressions are either between a person and the Makom, blessed be He, or between a person and his fellow. Through transgressions between a person and his fellow, a person is compared to a ram possessing horns, goring northward and southward to harm his fellow — whether in length of days, which is on the right, or in wealth and honor, which is on the left. Therefore he brings a ram as his substitute, or a lamb or a goat, as it is written (Genesis 22:13): ״וְהִנֵּה אַיִל אַחַר נֶאֱחַז בַּסְּבַךְ בְּקַרְנָיו״ — that is to say, this ram is other than the first, for this man — קַרְנֵי רְאֵם קַרְנָיו, with them he gores peoples. And by justice the sinner deserved to be judged by flaying and dissection and burning to ashes, but in HaShem's compassion upon him, He brought him out from the inversion, and another ram was taken as his substitute. And for this reason the ram was caught in the thicket by its horns specifically, for so too the person deserved to be seized for death on account of his horns, and he was ransomed from destruction by means of this ram, through the compassion of HaShem, blessed be He — and the substitute was a ram in place of a ram." "However, one who sins against Heaven is like one who had a visible horn between his eyes, rising straight upward, as though he wishes to gore upward, as it were. And this is along the lines of what Rashi explained in parashat לֶךְ לְךָ (Genesis 14:2) regarding שְׁמֵאֵבֶר. And therefore the Sages, whose memory is a blessing, said (Shabbat 28b): The ox that Adam the first man offered had one horn in its forehead. Whence do the Sages derive this? If it is because it is written (Psalms 69:32) ״מַקְרִין״ — written defectively — perhaps the horn was on one of the sides, and whence do we know it was in his forehead? Rather, it is certain that Adam the first man was alone in the world and sinned against no creature except HaShem alone; therefore Adam resembled the form of a bull that has a single horn in its forehead, for from there it rises upward. For so he did the deed of a beast and thought to be like Elohim, knowing good and evil — and that is a goring upward. Therefore he needed to bring his substitute: a bull bearing a single horn, and one happened to be available for him at that moment, so that the substitute would resemble from every aspect the form of the sinner and rebel." "And perhaps it is for this reason: there was available to him a bull whose horns precede its hooves (Ḥullin 60a), for the hooves are close to the earth and the way of an animal is to cause harm also with its leg and to ruin the earth — so too did man, through his sin, cause the ruin of the earth, as it is written (Genesis 3:17): ״אֲרוּרָה הָאֲדָמָה בַּעֲבוּרֶךָ״. However, the ruin of the earth was drawn from the sin of the horn that preceded it; therefore a bull whose horn preceded its hooves was made available to him. And by way of guidance, HaShem placed in his heart that all these judgments truly belonged to the sinner, and through this a person comes to a broken spirit — for this is the ultimate desired goal of the sacrifices of Elohim, as it is written (1 Samuel 15:22): ״הִנֵּה שְׁמֹעַ מִזֶּבַח טוֹב״, which the commentators explained to mean that the essential good drawn from the sacrifice is to hearken to the voice of HaShem, for HaShem did not command concerning matters of burnt offering and sacrifice in and of themselves and at the outset, but only for the sake of what is drawn from them — and that is, to hearken to the voice of HaShem. And concerning this it is written (Psalms 75:5–6): ״אָמַרְתִּי לַהוֹלְלִים אַל תָּהֹלּוּ וְלָרְשָׁעִים אַל תָּרִימוּ קָרֶן, אַל תָּרִימוּ לַמָּרוֹם קַרְנְכֶם״ — first קֶרֶן, singular, and afterward קַרְנְכֶם, plural, two in meaning. For first he warns them about the most severe sin — raising a single horn upward to rebel against the Holy One, blessed be He — and afterward he warns them not to sin one against another, with two horns turning to the right and to the left. Therefore he said ״תָּרִימוּ לַמָּרוֹם״ — doubled — and both are in the language of raising, for so is the way of an animal to raise its horns somewhat at the time of goring. And even though for the generations the commandment came that even for a sin between a person and the Makom one should bring an animal bearing two horns — that is after the giving of the Torah, when all Israel became guarantors for one another, and one who sins against Heaven causes the destruction of his fellows, like Achan who sinned alone yet caused the downfall of all Israel. And goring against creatures makes a greater impression than what one does upward, for if his transgressions multiplied, what can HaShem, blessed be He, do to him? But Adam the first man, who was alone and from whom no other person could receive harm — therefore he needed to bring one bearing a single horn, even though a goring upward is not as consequential." "And since everyone who is greater than his fellow — his goring makes a greater impression and it is harder to find a remedy for his wound, for it is great — therefore these divisions came among those who bring offerings. For the anointed priest and the Sanhedrin — their goring makes a great impression, whether in divine faiths or against their fellows — therefore their offering is a bull, whose goring is greater and more dangerous than the goring of a lamb or a goat. But any other individual — his goring is not so great, therefore his offering is a lamb or a goat or a ram — specifically in the case of a rich person, who answers with audacity and leans on the premise that 'my hand has found strength' and that his force is great, goring northward and southward, he will not turn back for anything. But the poor person — for his strength has failed, therefore his goring is not so great, and he is like a bird that strikes with its wings to the right and to the left, along the lines of שְׁמֵאֵבֶר — who placed a wing to fly and to spring and to rebel against the Holy One, blessed be He. Therefore his offering is turtledoves and young pigeons, for he himself is among the pursued, like the turtledoves, and even so he strikes at those beneath him, as it is written (Leviticus 26:36): ״וְרָדַף אֶתְכֶם קוֹל עָלֶה נִדָּף״. For the leaf itself is driven by the wind that strikes it, and even so it pursues and strikes at what is beneath it — as will be explained in its place. And since the wings are the place of the sin, therefore it is said: ״וְשִׁסַּע אֹתוֹ בִכְנָפָיו״. And in the case of extreme poverty, the person is as though dead, as if he has no vital soul within him — therefore his offering is a tenth of an ephah of fine flour, from the plant kingdom, which has no vital spirit at all."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "The flax-seed identification of Cain's offering follows Genesis Rabbah 22:5 and Rashi on Genesis 4:3; the Kli Yakar assumes the reader knows this background.", 4 "The reference to 'its end' (והשיב את הגזלה) likely points to Leviticus 5:23 in the same parashah; 'its middle' (והסיר את מראתו בנוצתה) to Leviticus 1:16.", 5 "The Makom is an epithet for Elohim; rendered by transliteration rather than translation per voice rules. The phrase קַרְנֵי רְאֵם קַרְנָיו alludes to Numbers 23:22; I preserved it as a Hebrew phrase without inserting the source citation he did not write.", 6 "I read שְׁמֵאֵבֶר (in Rashi on Gen. 14:2) as a place name; the Kli Yakar's point is that Rashi there connects a word to the concept of a horn (קֶרֶן/אֵבֶר), which he invokes here. The Talmudic citation (שבת כ״ח:) appears to reference Shabbat 28b.", 7 "The phrase וְיוֹתֵר שַׁיֶּכֶת (literally 'and more fitting/applicable') I rendered as 'makes a greater impression'; the argument is that sins between people have more tangible impact than sins against Heaven."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:36:00.941201331Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}